    "q-42923": {
      "dom_id": "step-q42923",
      "name": "Question 2",
      "problem": "Find the determinant of the matrix \n$F=[\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}].$",
      "worked_solution": "Notice that the $3$rd column contains three zeros. So, using Laplace expansion along the $3$rd column, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{det}(F) & =|\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -1 & -2 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =(-1)\\cdot (-1)^{3+3}|\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}| \\\\  & =-|\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}|\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe determinant of a triangular matrix is equal to the product of the entries on the main diagonal. Therefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-|\\begin{aligned}3 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -3\\end{aligned}| & =-1\\cdot (3\\cdot (-1)\\cdot (-3))=-9.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we obtain\n\n$$\ndet(F)=-9.\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
